---
title: "Building a writing lock screen for Wayland"
date: "2026-09-05"
summary: "A technical overview of Anvi, a Wayland lock screen that makes me write before returning to my laptop."
tags: ["wayland", "programming", "writing"]
published: true
---

I'm building a lock screen for Wayland that only lets you back into your computer after you have typed a certain number of words. The number of words will be configurable by the user. The idea is similar to Cold Turkey Writer, but implemented as a Wayland lock screen.

My motivation is simple: I want to create more than I consume, and writing is a good way of creating. Locking my screen frees me from all the distractions on my laptop. It unchains my brain from the invisible forces that prevent it from entering a writing flow state. When the only option is to write, writing becomes very enjoyable.

Here is a quick technical overview of what I have implemented so far and how it works.

## The current state

At the moment, I can lock the screen and type onto a black background. There is no line wrapping or much else that a proper text editor requires. It is very crude. Pressing `q` exits the lock screen; eventually, the exit condition will instead be reaching the required number of words.

## The main loop

At a high level, the application works like this:

1. Set up the application state.
2. Enter a loop.
3. Call `wl_display_dispatch`, which blocks until the compositor sends useful information.
4. Let libwayland invoke the necessary callbacks to update the application state.
5. Draw the screen based on the updated state.
6. Check the exit condition and release the lock if it has been fulfilled.

This flow is clearly visible in the relatively simple `main.c`. Most of the application's real logic lives in its callback functions and their helpers.

## Globals and callbacks

The first thing a Wayland application needs to do is register globals. Globals are the objects and services advertised by the compositor.

When using libwayland, the application then needs to handle callbacks. Libwayland invokes the appropriate callback when, for example, a key or modifier is pressed or the screen geometry changes. These callbacks contain the core of the application's logic.

## One interesting detail

Most communication between the compositor and a client application happens through Unix sockets. The pixels to be drawn, however, are communicated through a shared memory pool. Sending that amount of data continuously through Unix sockets would be too slow.

The lock screen is still primitive, but the important foundation now works: it can take control of the screen, receive keyboard input, draw content, and eventually release the lock when a condition is met.
